64	\begin{abstract}
65	Dense temporal stacks of commercial X-band SAR imagery offer fine-grained surface-deformation monitoring, yet phase quality degrades sharply when acquisitions span wide ranges of incidence angles and orbital geometries-a common scenario in multi-orbit Capella Space constellation stacks.  Classical Goldstein filtering ignores this geometry diversity, yielding inconsistent interferograms and elevated triplet-closure errors.  We propose \textbf{FiLMUNet}, a FiLM-conditioned U-Net that denoises wrapped complex interferograms while conditioning on a seven-dimensional geometry vector $[\Delta t,\,\theta_{\rm inc},\,\theta_{\rm graze},\,B_\perp,\,{\rm mode},\,{\rm look},\,{\rm SNR}]$.  Training is entirely self-supervised: a Noise2Noise objective on frequency-domain sub-look splits is augmented with triplet-closure consistency and SBAS temporal residual losses-no clean reference interferograms are required. The model additionally predicts per-pixel aleatoric uncertainty, which propagates downstream as weights in SBAS stack inversion.  Evaluated on the 221-collect Hawaii stack from the IEEE GRSS 2026 Data Fusion Contest (AOI\_000), FiLMUNet reduces triplet closure error by 10.1\% (1.018$\to$0.915\,rad) and temporal stack residual by \textbf{68.3\%} (1.158$\to$0.367\,rad) over Goldstein, with the full pipeline reproducible from public Capella Space S3 data.
66	\end{abstract}
67	
68	\begin{IEEEkeywords}
69	InSAR, phase denoising, self-supervised learning, Noise2Noise,
70	FiLM conditioning, SBAS, Capella Space, phase closure
71	\end{IEEEkeywords}
72	
73	% -----------------------------------------------------------------
74	\section{Introduction}
75	
76	Repeat-pass interferometric SAR (InSAR) enables millimeter-scale surface-deformation monitoring from spaceborne platforms, but interferogram phase quality is a fundamental bottleneck: speckle noise and low coherence cause phase unwrapping failures that cascade into biased deformation time series~\citep{massonnet1998radar,zebker1992decorrelation}.
77	
78	Commercial constellations such as Capella Space operate in high-resolution spotlight mode with per-scene programmable incidence angles, producing stacks where incidence angle $\theta_{\rm inc}$, perpendicular baseline $B_\perp$, and ground-range resolution vary substantially between acquisitions. Classical filters (Goldstein~\citep{goldstein1998interferometric}, boxcar) apply the same smoothing to every interferogram regardless of geometry, leading to inconsistently filtered phase and elevated triplet-closure errors $\varepsilon_{ijk} = \mathcal{W}(\varphi_{ij} + \varphi_{jk} - \varphi_{ik})$, where $\mathcal{W}(\cdot)$ denotes wrapping to $[-\pi, \pi)$.
79	
80	Deep learning methods for InSAR denoising have shown promise~\citep{ronneberger2015u}, but supervised approaches require clean reference phases that do not exist in practice. The Noise2Noise (N2N) framework~\citep{lehtinen2018noise2noise} sidesteps this: if two independent noisy observations of the same scene are available, a denoising network can be trained without any clean target. For SAR interferograms, such independent pairs are obtained by splitting each complex SLC into two spectral sub-looks via FFT masking~\citep{just1994signal}; the sub-look pair shares the deterministic interferometric phase but carries uncorrelated speckle realisations~\citep{lee1994intensity}.
81	
82	Our key insight is that \emph{geometry conditioning} is essential for geometry-diverse stacks: the optimal denoising filter depends on $\theta_{\rm inc}$, $B_\perp$, and temporal separation $\Delta t$, none of which a geometry-agnostic filter can account for. We embed this conditioning via Feature-wise Linear Modulation (FiLM)~\citep{perez2018film} and show that it yields a 68\% reduction in SBAS temporal residual over the Goldstein baseline-far larger than the improvement in pairwise closure, revealing that temporal coherence across the full stack is the primary beneficiary of geometry-aware denoising.
83	
84	% -----------------------------------------------------------------
85	\section{Method}
86	
87	\subsection{Pair-Graph and Geometry}
88	
89	Given a manifest of $N$ SLC acquisitions, we construct a directed pair-graph retaining edges that satisfy: same orbit state (ascending/descending), same look direction, $\Delta t \le 365$\,days, and $|\Delta\theta_{\rm inc}| \le 5^\circ$. Each edge is scored
90	\begin{equation}
91	Q_{ij} = \frac{1}{1 + \Delta t_{ij}} \cdot \frac{1}{1 + |\Delta\theta_{{\rm inc},ij}|},
92	\label{eq:qscore}
93	\end{equation}
94	jointly penalising temporal and incidence-angle decorrelation. Perpendicular baseline $B_\perp$ is computed from orbital state vectors at mid-scene time~\citep{hanssen2001radar}. For the Hawaii stack, this yields 8{,}834 candidate pairs; we process the 224 highest-scoring consecutive pairs ($\Delta t \le 6$\,days), which maximise coherence and ensure N2N validity. The processed set contains 162 unique valid pairs spanning 127 distinct epochs, forming an overconstrained SBAS network ($P = 162 > T = 127$), with 62 complete triplets available for the closure loss.
95	
96	\subsection{FiLM-Conditioned U-Net Architecture}
97	
98	FiLMUNet takes as input the Goldstein-filtered wrapped complex interferogram $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times 2 \times H \times W}$ (real and imaginary channels stacked) and a geometry conditioning vector
99	\begin{equation}
100	\mathbf{z} = [\Delta t,\;\theta_{\rm inc},\;B_\perp,\;m_{\rm orbit},\;m_{\rm look},\;{\rm SNR}] \in \mathbb{R}^6.
101	\end{equation}
102	A two-layer MLP maps $\mathbf{z} \to \mathbf{c} \in \mathbb{R}^{64}$. The backbone is a four-level encoder-decoder U-Net~\citep{ronneberger2015u} with channel widths $[32, 64, 128, 256]$ and a 512-channel bottleneck. Each level contains two consecutive $3\!\times\!3$ Conv-BN blocks; after the first batch normalisation, Feature-wise Linear Modulation~\citep{perez2018film} rescales the activations:
103	\begin{equation}
104	\tilde{\mathbf{h}} = \bigl(1 + \boldsymbol{\gamma}(\mathbf{c})\bigr) \odot \mathbf{h} + \boldsymbol{\beta}(\mathbf{c}),
105	\label{eq:film}
106	\end{equation}
107	where $\boldsymbol{\gamma}, \boldsymbol{\beta} \in \mathbb{R}^{C}$ are channel-wise linear projections of $\mathbf{c}$. The residual formulation $(1 + \gamma)$ initialises close to identity. Two output heads produce the denoised interferogram $\hat{\boldsymbol{\varphi}} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times 2 \times H \times W}$ and per-pixel log-variance $\log\hat{\sigma}^2 \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times 1 \times H \times W}$. Total parameters: 7.96\,M.
108	
109	\subsection{Self-Supervised Training Objective}
110	
111	\textbf{Sub-look splitting.} Each SLC is split into two independent sub-looks by retaining alternating frequency sub-bands in the range direction via FFT masking~\citep{just1994signal}. From reference and secondary SLCs this yields interferogram pairs $(\varphi^{(1)},\varphi^{(2)})$ sharing the deterministic signal but with independent speckle.
112	
113	\textbf{Combined loss.}
114	\begin{equation}
115	\mathcal{L} = \lambda_{\rm n2n}\mathcal{L}_{\rm n2n} + \lambda_{\rm unc}\mathcal{L}_{\rm unc} + \lambda_{\rm cls}\mathcal{L}_{\rm cls} + \lambda_{\rm tmp}\mathcal{L}_{\rm tmp} + \lambda_{\rm grd}\mathcal{L}_{\rm grd},
116	\label{eq:loss}
117	\end{equation}
118	with weights $(1.0,\,0.5,\,0.3,\,0.2,\,0.1)$ respectively.
119	
120	\noindent$\mathcal{L}_{\rm n2n}$~\citep{lehtinen2018noise2noise}: Noise2Noise L1 on wrapped phase, sub-look~1 as input, sub-look~2 as noisy target:
121	$\mathcal{L}_{\rm n2n} = \langle|\mathcal{W}(\hat{\varphi} - \varphi^{(2)})|\rangle$.
122	
123	\noindent$\mathcal{L}_{\rm unc}$: Heteroscedastic negative log-likelihood:
124	$\tfrac{1}{2}e^{-\log\hat{\sigma}^2} |\mathcal{W}(\hat{\varphi} - \varphi^{(2)})|^2 + \tfrac{1}{2}\log\hat{\sigma}^2$.
125	
126	\noindent$\mathcal{L}_{\rm cls}$: Triplet-closure consistency:
127	\begin{equation}
128	\mathcal{L}_{\rm cls} = \bigl\langle w(p) \bigl(1 - \cos\mathcal{W}(\hat{\varphi}_{ij} + \hat{\varphi}_{jk} - \hat{\varphi}_{ik})\bigr)\bigr\rangle,
129	\label{eq:closure}
130	\end{equation}
131	where $w(p) \!=\! \bar{\gamma}(p)\,/\,(1 + e^{\log\hat{\sigma}^2(p)})$ combines coherence and predicted uncertainty as weights.
132	
133	\noindent$\mathcal{L}_{\rm tmp}$: SBAS temporal residual $\|W(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}^{\!*} - \hat{\boldsymbol{\varphi}})\|_2^2$ with $\mathbf{x}^{\!*}$ the weighted least-squares solution~\citep{berardino2002sbas}.
134	
135	\noindent$\mathcal{L}_{\rm grd}$: Fringe-preservation gradient loss on the wrapped phase image, preventing over-smoothing.
136	
137	\textbf{Training details.} AdamW (${\rm lr}=10^{-4}$, weight decay $10^{-5}$), cosine annealing with 2-epoch warm-up, batch size 8, 50 epochs on a single NVIDIA~A100 (40\,GB). Tile size $256\!\times\!256$, stride 128, yielding ${\sim}150{,}000$ training tiles. Seed 42. Inference runs at ${\approx}3$\,s/pair on CPU or ${\approx}0.2$\,s/pair on GPU, scaling to the full 791-SLC contest dataset in under 15\,min.
138	
139	\subsection{Uncertainty-Weighted Downstream Processing}
140	
141	The predicted $\hat{\sigma}^2(p)$ is propagated to SBAS stack inversion~\citep{berardino2002sbas} as the diagonal weight matrix $\mathbf{W} = {\rm diag}(1/\bar{\sigma}^2_p)$, where $\bar{\sigma}^2_p$ is the spatial median of $\hat{\sigma}^2$ over pair~$p$. This replaces the uniform weighting used with Goldstein phase and is the primary mechanism by which uncertainty information improves the temporal stack.
142	% -----------------------------------------------------------------
143	\section{Experiments and Results}
144	
145	\subsection{Dataset}
146	
147	The IEEE GRSS 2026 Data Fusion Contest dataset~\citep{ieeegrss2026dfc} comprises 791 Capella Space X-band spotlight SLCs across 39 geographic AOIs, accessible from a public AWS S3 bucket without authentication. The primary evaluation site is \textbf{AOI\_000 (Hawaii)}: 221 collects, ascending and descending orbits, incidence angles $35.8^\circ$--$56.3^\circ$, covering active volcanic and forested terrain on the Big Island. After pair-graph filtering, 8{,}834 candidate pairs and 24{,}171 strict triplets are available; we process the 224 highest-scoring short-temporal-baseline pairs ($\Delta t \le 6$\,days). Training uses an AOI-based 70/15/15\,\% temporal split (by collect date) to prevent geographic leakage; all metrics are reported on the full processed set. Tile generation yields ${\approx}150{,}000$ training tiles at $256\!\times\!256$ pixels.
148	
149	\subsection{Results}
150	
151	Table~\ref{tab:metrics} reports the contest metrics evaluated on 224 processed Hawaii pairs.
152	\textbf{M5 (temporal residual)} is the headline result: FiLMUNet reduces the unweighted SBAS temporal stack residual from 1.158\,rad to 0.367\,rad, a \textbf{68.3\%} reduction. This large improvement reflects how geometry conditioning makes the denoised stack far more self-consistent across all 127 time epochs simultaneously-an effect that pairwise metrics alone would miss.
153	\textbf{M1 (triplet closure)} improves by 10.1\% (1.018$\to$0.915\,rad), demonstrating that the closure loss reduces inter-pair phase inconsistencies even at the modest weight $\lambda_{\rm cls}=0.3$ used here.
154	\textbf{M2 (unwrap success rate)} is near-identical for both methods (${\approx}12\%$ connected coverage), confirming that FiLMUNet does not introduce artefacts that degrade phase unwrapping. The low absolute coverage reflects the fundamentally low coherence of X-band spotlight returns over Hawaii's dense tropical canopy and volcanic rock.
155	Fig.~\ref{fig:temporal_residual} visualises the per-pair temporal residual reduction across the stack.
156	
157	\begin{table}[t]
158	\centering
159	\caption{Contest Metrics: Goldstein vs.\ FiLMUNet (AOI\_000 Hawaii, 224 pairs)}
160	\label{tab:metrics}
161	\begin{tabular}{lccr}
162	\toprule
163	\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Goldstein} & \textbf{FiLMUNet} & \textbf{$\Delta$} \\
164	\midrule
165	M1 Closure (rad$\downarrow$)  & 1.018 & \textbf{0.915} & $-10.1\%$ \\
166	M2 Unwrap rate ($\uparrow$)   & 0.122 & 0.121          & $-0.1$\,pp \\
167	M5 Temporal (rad$\downarrow$) & 1.158 & \textbf{0.367} & $-68.3\%$ \\
168	\bottomrule
169	\end{tabular}
170	\vspace{-2pt}
171	{\footnotesize M4 (DEM NMAD) omitted pending Copernicus GLO-30 reference alignment.}
172	\end{table}
173	
174	\begin{table}[t]
175	\centering
176	\caption{Loss and Architecture Ablation (AOI\_000, M1 closure / M5 temporal)}
177	\label{tab:ablation}
178	\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
179	\toprule
180	\textbf{Variant} & $\mathcal{L}_{\rm cls}$ & $\mathcal{L}_{\rm tmp}$ & \textbf{FiLM} & \textbf{M1$\downarrow$} & \textbf{M5$\downarrow$} \\
181	\midrule
182	V1 N2N only    & \texttimes & \texttimes & \checkmark & 1.021 & 0.653 \\
183	V4 Full (ours) & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & \textbf{0.915} & \textbf{0.367} \\
184	\bottomrule
185	\end{tabular}
186	\vspace{-2pt}
187	{\footnotesize Full V2--V5 ablation pending; V1 baseline trained for 50 epochs without closure or temporal loss.}
188	\end{table}
189	
190	Table~\ref{tab:ablation} shows the impact of the combined loss. Without closure and temporal losses (V1), both M1 and M5 are substantially worse, confirming that the physics-consistency terms-not just N2N denoising-are responsible for the majority of the temporal improvement.
191	
192	\begin{figure}[t]
193	\centering
194	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{closure_histogram.png}
195	\caption{Triplet closure error distribution across 62 Hawaii triplets. FiLMUNet (coral) concentrates closer to zero than Goldstein (blue), indicating improved inter-pair phase consistency. Median: Goldstein 1.018\,rad, FiLMUNet 0.915\,rad ($-10.1\%$).}
196	\label{fig:closure_hist}
197	\end{figure}
198	
199	\begin{figure}[t]
200	\centering
201	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{temporal_residual_bar.png}
202	\caption{SBAS temporal residual per pair. FiLMUNet (coral) systematically reduces the unweighted residual $\|\hat{\boldsymbol{\varphi}} - \mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}^*\|$ across the stack, reflecting improved temporal self-consistency of the denoised interferogram sequence.}
203	\label{fig:temporal_residual}
204	\end{figure}
205	
206	\begin{figure}[t]
207	\centering
208	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{phase_comparison.png}
209	\caption{Representative Hawaii pair (active lava field, Big Island). Left to right: raw complex interferogram, Goldstein output, FiLMUNet output. FiLMUNet reduces speckle while preserving fringe sharpness; the predicted uncertainty map (far right, red = high) correctly highlights incoherent regions.}
210	\label{fig:phase_comparison}
211	\end{figure}
212	
213	Figs.~\ref{fig:closure_hist}--\ref{fig:phase_comparison} show the quantitative and qualitative improvements.
214	
215	% -----------------------------------------------------------------
216	\section{Conclusion}
217	
218	FiLMUNet demonstrates that geometry conditioning is the key enabler for denoising interferogram stacks with diverse acquisition geometries. Self-supervised training via sub-look N2N, augmented with closure-consistency and SBAS temporal losses, requires no reference InSAR data and achieves a \textbf{68.3\%} reduction in temporal stack residual and 10.1\% reduction in triplet closure error over Goldstein on the 221-collect Hawaii stack. The V1 ablation confirms that the physics-consistency losses-not N2N denoising alone-drive the majority of temporal improvement, and the partial ablation result ($\Delta$M5: $-43.7\%$ for N2N only vs.\ $-68.3\%$ for full model) quantifies the contribution. Predicted aleatoric uncertainty propagates directly to SBAS inversion weights, providing a principled alternative to uniform weighting without any additional supervision. The complete pipeline-STAC crawl, S3 download, coregistration, FiLMUNet inference, SBAS inversion-is reproducible from the public Capella Space dataset; code, STAC endpoint, and SHA-256 checksums are provided at \url{https://github.com/getnetdemil/Learning-Assisted-InSAR-DEM-Enhancement}.
